.TH VIRCON32 1 "October 11, 2026" "Vircon32 Emulator 26.02.18" "Vircon32 Emulator"
.SH NAME
Vircon32 \- emulator for the Vircon32 32-bit virtual game console
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B Vircon32
.RI [ cartridge.v32 ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Vircon32
runs games for Vircon32, a 32-bit virtual console designed to be simple
to program while being roughly as capable as the 32-bit home consoles
of the 1990s, limited to 2D graphics. Games come as ROM files
.RI ( .v32 ),
built with the Vircon32 development tools (see
.BR compile (1)
and
.BR packrom (1))
or downloaded.
.PP
Given a
.IR cartridge.v32 ,
the emulator inserts it and powers the console on. Without one, it
starts with the console off and no cartridge; use the
.B Cartridge
menu, or open a
.I .v32
file with the emulator from a file manager. More than one argument is
an error.
.PP
Like a real console, the cartridge slot locks while the power is on:
power the console off before removing or changing a cartridge.
.SS The console
.TS
l l.
Screen	640 \(mu 360 pixels, true color with alpha, 60 frames per second
CPU	32-bit, 16 registers, 15 million instructions per second
Memory	16 MB of RAM (4 Mi words of 32 bits)
Graphics	up to 256 textures of 1024 \(mu 1024 pixels, drawn as regions
	with scaling, rotation and blending
Sound	16 channels, stereo 16-bit at 44100 Hz, up to 1024 sounds
Gamepads	4 ports; D-pad, A, B, X, Y, L, R and Start
Saving	memory cards of 1 MB (256 Ki words)
Cartridges	up to 128 Mi words of program, plus textures and sounds
.TE
.SH THE WINDOW
The menu bar is hidden while playing. It appears when the mouse
pointer is over the window, and the
.B Escape
key shows or hides it. While the window is minimized or loses focus,
emulation pauses.
.SS Menus
.TP
.B Console
Power on/off, Reset, Save state, Load state, the savestate slot (1 to
4), and Quit.
.TP
.B Cartridge
Load, unload or change the cartridge (only while the power is off),
and a list of recently used cartridges.
.TP
.B Memory Card
The card in use. When memory cards are handled manually (see
.BR "MEMORY CARDS" ),
also: create an empty card, load, unload or change the card, and a
list of recent cards.
.TP
.B Gamepads
For each of the 4 gamepads, the device that controls it: no device,
the keyboard, or a joystick profile made with
.BR EditControls (1).
The keyboard can be assigned to only one gamepad; a joystick profile
can be assigned to several gamepads when several identical joysticks
are connected.
.TP
.B Options
Window size (1\(mu, 2\(mu or 3\(mu the console resolution), full
screen, sound volume (0 to 100), mute, memory card handling,
language (English or Spanish), and save screenshot.
.TP
.B Help
A quick guide, the Readme file, and version information.
.SS Keyboard shortcuts
.TS
lb l.
Escape	show or hide the menu bar
Ctrl+P	power on / off
Ctrl+R, F5	reset
Ctrl+L	load or change the cartridge (power off only)
Ctrl+U	unload the cartridge
F2	save state in the current slot
F4	load state from the current slot
Ctrl+S	save a screenshot
Ctrl+1, Ctrl+2, Ctrl+3	window size 1\(mu, 2\(mu, 3\(mu
Ctrl+F	full screen
Ctrl+M	mute
Ctrl+Q	quit
.TE
.SH CONTROLS
By default only gamepad 1 is connected, controlled by the keyboard:
.PP
.TS
l l.
D-pad	arrow keys
L, R	Q, W
Y, X	A, S
B, A	Z, X
Start	Return
.TE
.PP
Keys and joysticks are configured with
.BR EditControls (1).
.SS The Command button
Every keyboard or joystick profile may define an extra button, the
Command button, which is not part of the console's gamepad. Held down
together with a gamepad button, it controls the emulator from any of
the four gamepads, which is useful in arcade cabinets and front-ends:
.PP
.TS
lb l.
Command + Start	quit the emulator
Command + X	reset
Command + L	save state
Command + R	load state
.TE
.SH MEMORY CARDS
Games save their data on a memory card. There are two ways to handle
cards, chosen in
.BR "Options \(-> Memory card handling" :
.TP
.B Automatic
(the default) Every cartridge gets its own card, created the first
time it is played:
.IR Cards/game.memc ,
named after the cartridge file.
.TP
.B Manual
No card is inserted unless the player creates or loads one from the
.B Memory Card
menu, as with a physical console. Use the right card for each game:
a game is supposed to check the signature at the start of the card
before using it, but nothing guarantees that it does.
.SH SAVESTATES
A savestate records the whole state of the console so that play can be
resumed later from that exact moment, even in games that cannot save.
Each cartridge has 4 slots, stored as
.IR Savestates/game_Slot N .state .
Saving or loading cannot be undone, so check the selected slot.
.PP
Savestates do not include the screen contents, which Vircon32 keeps
between frames: after loading one, a game that does not redraw the
whole screen every frame may show stale graphics until it does.
.SH V32IO DEVICES
The emulator built from the
.B v32io
branch of
.I https://github.com/wedge1020/ComputerSoftware
(or the stock sources with the patches from the v32io repository)
offers two more devices in the
.B Gamepads
menu. They connect the host computer's keyboard and mouse to programs
that use the v32io drivers (see
.BR keyboard.hpp (3v32)
and
.BR mouse.hpp (3v32)),
by encoding them on the 11 controls of a gamepad port. To any other
program they look like a gamepad giving meaningless input.
.TP
.B v32kbd
The host keyboard, reporting one key event per frame. It takes every
key, so it cannot be used together with the ordinary
.B Keyboard
device on any gamepad. The emulator's own shortcuts keep working and
are also sent to the program.
.TP
.B v32mouse
The host mouse. A left click on the game screen captures the pointer
(the click itself is not sent to the program); left
.B Ctrl
+ left
.B Alt
(left
.B Control
+ left
.B Option
on a Mac) releases it and shows the menus. While the pointer is
captured,
.B Escape
goes to the program instead of toggling the menu bar. When v32mouse is
in use, the
.B Gamepads
menu also has a
.B "v32mouse speed"
setting: how many host mouse counts make one step of movement (1, 2,
3, 4, 6 or 8; default 2).
.PP
Only one gamepad can use each of the two devices.
.SH FILES
All files live in the emulator's own folder, the directory of the
.B Vircon32
executable:
.I /usr/local/Vircon32/Emulator
with the default Linux and macOS installation. The emulator writes to
it, so it must be writable by the user.
.TP
.I Bios/StandardBios.v32
The console BIOS. Another BIOS file in
.I Bios/
can be selected in the settings file.
.TP
.I Config\-Settings.xml
The settings: language, BIOS file, window size and full screen, volume
and mute, the number of audio buffers (more buffers avoid crackling at
the cost of latency), the device of each gamepad, memory card
handling, the savestate slot, the last folders used, and the recent
cartridges and cards. The v32io build also stores the v32mouse speed
here. The file is rewritten on exit.
.TP
.I Config\-Controls.xml
The keyboard profile and the joystick profiles, as written by
.BR EditControls (1).
.TP
.I Cards/
Memory cards created in automatic mode
.RI ( .memc
files: the signature
.B V32\-MEMC
followed by the card's 262144 words).
.TP
.I Savestates/
Savestates.
.TP
.I Screenshots/
Screenshots, named after the date and time they were taken
.RI ( "2026-10-11 10.32.00.png" ).
.TP
.I DebugLog.txt
A log of the last run, useful when the emulator fails to start.
.TP
.IR GuiFont.ttf ", " Images/ ", " Readme.txt
The menu font, window icon and \(lqno signal\(rq screen, and the
Readme shown from the
.B Help
menu.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The emulator was closed normally.
.TP
.B 1
More than one argument was given, or the emulator could not start;
errors after start-up are shown in a dialog box.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR EditControls (1),
.BR packrom (1),
.BR compile (1),
.BR vircon32 (3v32)
.PP
The Vircon32 website:
.I https://www.vircon32.com
